-- Q3554: System Availability and Change Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provider B is more reliable because its faster 1-hour MTTR is the only metric that matters for system availability","Provider A offers greater reliability because its 2,160-hour MTBF means approximately 90 days between failures versus Provider B''s 30 days","Both providers offer identical reliability because MTBF and MTTR are inversely proportional and cancel each other out","Neither provider can be compared because MTBF only applies to hardware components, not to cloud hosting services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 and ISO/IEC 27031, MTBF measures average operational time between failures. Provider A''s 2,160-hour MTBF (90 days) means far fewer disruptions than Provider B''s 720-hour MTBF (30 days), making Provider A more reliable despite slower recovery.
Wrong (A): MTTR measures restoration speed after failure but ignores failure frequency. A system that fails three times more often is less reliable even with faster recovery.
Wrong (C): MTBF and MTTR measure different aspects of availability and do not mathematically cancel each other.
Wrong (D): MTBF applies to any system with measurable uptime, including cloud services, not just physical hardware.$EXPL$
