-- Q3556: System Availability and Change Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["RTO should be set below MTD to provide a safety margin in case recovery encounters unexpected complications","MTD and RTO should always equal each other because they measure the same recovery target from different perspectives","RTO should exceed MTD to allow extra time for thorough system testing before declaring the service fully recovered","MTD and RTO are different names for the same metric, so setting them equal is technically redundant but harmless"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 3.4, MTD is the absolute maximum time a process can be unavailable before unacceptable damage occurs. RTO must be set below MTD to provide a buffer, ensuring recovery completes before the tolerance threshold is breached even if complications arise.
Wrong (B): MTD and RTO measure different things. MTD is the business tolerance limit; RTO is the technical recovery target. Setting them equal eliminates any safety margin.
Wrong (C): Setting RTO above MTD guarantees the organization will exceed its maximum tolerable downtime, defeating the purpose of continuity planning.
Wrong (D): MTD and RTO are distinct metrics with different definitions and purposes, not synonyms.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3556;

