-- Q3562: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Back-out plans are unnecessary for well-tested production changes","An untested rollback mechanism may fail when needed, creating false security","Production changes rarely fail, so back-out testing is a low-priority activity","Corrupt backups are acceptable as long as the original change succeeds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06.01, back-out plans must be tested before production changes to validate that the rollback mechanism actually works when needed. An untested rollback creates a false sense of security because the plan is only as reliable as its last successful test.
Wrong (A): All production changes carry risk regardless of prior testing, and COBIT requires validated rollback procedures for every change.
Wrong (C): Production failures, while not the majority, occur frequently enough that untested rollback plans pose a significant operational risk.
Wrong (D): Corrupt backups cannot serve as a viable rollback path, and accepting them undermines the entire purpose of back-out planning.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3562;

-- Q3563: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Data compression, which reduces file sizes to save storage space","RAID, which replicates data across multiple storage devices for fault tolerance","Data deduplication, which eliminates redundant copies to optimize capacity","File archiving, which moves inactive data to long-term offline storage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 Section 5.1.2, RAID distributes and replicates data across multiple storage devices to provide fault tolerance. Different RAID levels (1, 5, 6, 10) offer mirroring and striping with parity, ensuring data remains accessible if one drive fails.
Wrong (A): Data compression reduces storage consumption but provides no redundancy or fault tolerance if a device fails.
Wrong (C): Deduplication eliminates duplicate data to save space but does not replicate data across multiple devices for availability.
Wrong (D): File archiving moves data to long-term storage for retention purposes but does not provide real-time fault tolerance.$EXPL$
