-- Q3573: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Zero data loss on failover because every transaction is confirmed on both servers","Faster query performance and reduced response times for end users","Reduced storage costs through real-time data compression and deduplication","Elimination of the need for user authentication on the standby server"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 Section 5.2, synchronous mirroring confirms every write on both primary and mirror before acknowledging the transaction. This achieves RPO of zero, meaning no committed data is lost if the primary fails and the mirror is activated.
Wrong (B): Synchronous mirroring actually adds slight write latency due to dual-confirmation; it does not improve query performance.
Wrong (C): Data mirroring duplicates data for redundancy, increasing rather than reducing storage consumption.
Wrong (D): Authentication requirements are independent of the replication strategy and are not affected by mirroring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3573;

-- Q3574: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Staging environments do not need to match production configurations closely","Patches tested in staging may fail in production when environments have diverged","Configuration drift improves security by introducing environmental diversity","The staging environment should intentionally run older versions than production"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-128 Section 3.3, configuration drift between environments undermines testing reliability. When staging and production diverge in OS versions, libraries, or settings, a patch passing staging tests may fail in production due to undetected differences.
Wrong (A): Staging must closely match production to ensure test results accurately predict production behavior; mismatches invalidate testing.
Wrong (C): Environmental diversity does not improve security; drift introduces unpredictability and false confidence in pre-deployment testing.
Wrong (D): Running older versions in staging would guarantee that test results do not reflect production conditions, defeating the purpose.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3574;

-- Q3580: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
