-- Q3605: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Outdated policies are automatically rendered legally unenforceable after three years","Gaps emerge between documented controls and actual practices as threats and technology evolve","A standard review cycle is three years, so the current timeline raises no concern","Policy age has no meaningful effect on the organization''s overall security posture"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 (PM-1), security policies should be reviewed at least annually to reflect changes in threats, technology, and business operations. Stale policies create gaps between documented expectations and actual practices, leading to inconsistent control implementation.
Wrong (A): Policies do not automatically become unenforceable based on age alone; the risk is practical divergence from current operations, not legal invalidity.
Wrong (C): The rapidly evolving threat landscape demands at minimum annual reviews; a three-year cycle is insufficient for maintaining effective security posture.
Wrong (D): Policy currency directly affects security posture because outdated policies fail to address current threats, technologies, and business processes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3605;

-- Q3607: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Standards are mandatory requirements; guidelines are recommended practices allowing flexibility","Standards are optional recommendations; guidelines are mandatory compliance requirements","There is no meaningful difference between security standards and guidelines","Guidelines require regulatory approval; standards are developed internally only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-12 Rev. 1 Section 4.4, standards define mandatory requirements that support policies and must be followed by all applicable personnel and systems. Guidelines provide recommended practices that allow flexibility and alternative approaches when justified.
Wrong (B): This reverses the relationship. Standards are mandatory and guidelines are flexible, not the other way around.
Wrong (C): Standards and guidelines serve distinct purposes in a security program hierarchy, with different levels of compliance obligation.
Wrong (D): Both standards and guidelines can be developed internally or externally; neither requires specific regulatory approval to be valid.$EXPL$
